<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pfalzgrafenstein Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1327">1327</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pfalzgrafenstein Castle is a toll castle on a small island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaub">Kaub</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Louis IV the Bavarian built its tower in 1326–1327 to collect river tolls, and a surrounding curtain wall was added soon after. It remained a toll station of the counts palatine and their successors into the nineteenth century, and is now part of the Upper Middle Rhine Valley World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pfalzgrafenstein-castle.jpg" alt="Pfalzgrafenstein Castle" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jörg Braukmann · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burg_Pfalzgrafenstein_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the vineyards of the Haardt and the Rhine valley, and especially from the Rhine tolls collected at places such as Bacharach and Kaub, where the island toll castle of <b>Pfalzgrafenstein</b> still stands, which made the electors among the richest princes of the Empire in the later Middle Ages.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
